            <ArticleTitle>Portal vein thrombosis as the first sign of nephrotic syndrome.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>342-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1038/ncpneph0810</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">A 52-year-old man presented to hospital having experienced abdominal pain, abdominal distention and oliguria for 1 week. An abdominal CT scan revealed thrombosis in the superior mesenteric vein and in the right branch and the trunk of the portal vein.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INVESTIGATIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Physical examination, blood and urine analyses, color Doppler ultrasonography and abdominal CT scan.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DIAGNOSIS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Nephrotic syndrome complicated by portal vein thrombosis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MANAGEMENT" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Treatment with batroxobin, low-molecular-weight heparin, prostaglandin E, dipyridamole and methylprednisolone.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
